## Default Methods in Interfaces

### The Pattern

```xiom
interface Playable {
  fn play(&mut self);
  fn pause(&mut self);

  fn stop(&mut self) {
    self.pause();
  }
}
```

`stop()` has a default body. Any type implementing `Playable` automatically gets `stop()` for free. If a type wants custom stop behavior, it can override the default.

### Complete Example

```xiom
interface Greeter {
  fn name(&self) -> Str;

  fn greet(&self) -> Str {
    let base = "Hello, ";
    string.str_concat(base, self.name())
  }

  fn farewell(&self) -> Str {
    let base = "Goodbye, ";
    string.str_concat(base, self.name())
  }
}

type User { username: Str; }
fn User.name(&self) -> Str { self.username.clone() }

// User automatically has greet() and farewell()!
```

### When to Use Default Methods

- When behavior can be expressed in terms of other interface methods
- For convenience methods that all implementors would write the same way
- To add new methods to existing interfaces without breaking implementors

### Override When Needed

```xiom
fn Admin.greet(&self) -> Str {
  "Welcome back, Administrator"
}
```

Admin overrides `greet()` with a custom message while keeping the default `farewell()`.

